// The one unsafe way to make a view of memory.
//
// Everything else in the language produces a slice from something whose
// extent it already knows: an array converts to one, and a slice of a
// slice keeps the bound. This is for the other case -- bytes that came
// from outside, from a `read` or from an allocator -- where how many there
// are is something the caller knows and the compiler cannot.
//
// It has no body on purpose. A slice is a pointer and a length, so there
// is nothing to do but say so, and the compiler does that at the call: no
// call is emitted and nothing is copied.

/// A view of `length` bytes at `data`.
///
/// `unsafe` because nothing here can check that there are that many, or
/// that they stay there. The caller promises both.
offer fun BytesFromRaw(data: *u8, length: int) -> []u8 uses unsafe
